# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows checkouts here, so text files land in
# the working tree with CRLF while the repository stores LF. That is fine until
# something reads a file's bytes: a migration test matched a statement spanning
# a line break, "payment\nSET", against a file checked out as "payment\r\nSET",
# and failed on Windows while passing on CI for SQL that was never wrong.
#
# `text=auto` fixes what enters the repository, not what lands on disk: it
# guarantees LF in every commit whatever the contributor's platform, so CRLF
# can never be stored. Checkout still follows core.autocrlf, so this changes no
# file in an existing working tree and produces no diff.
#
# `eol=lf` is the stronger rule, forcing LF on disk too. It is worth the churn
# only where CRLF actually breaks something -- a Dockerfile's RUN lines and any
# shell script, both of which run on Linux. It is deliberately NOT applied to
# *.sql: that would rewrite 107 files in every working tree to spare one test
# an assumption it should not have made anyway. Read bytes with line endings
# normalised instead.
* text=auto

# Executed on Linux, where a trailing \r is a syntax error or a bad
# interpreter. No .sh files exist today; the rule is here so the first one is
# correct on arrival.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch the bytes. text=auto detects most of these already,
# being explicit costs nothing and a mangled PNG is a confusing bug.
*.png binary
*.psd filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
